1. What you need

You need:

WSL Ubuntu
Ansible installed in WSL
One Ubuntu EC2 instance
EC2 public IP
EC2 username, usually ubuntu
Password authentication enabled on EC2
Security Group allowing SSH port 22

Important: AWS Ubuntu EC2 normally uses SSH keys and password login is disabled by default. We will first enable password authentication.

Step 1 — Create an Ubuntu EC2

Create an Ubuntu EC2 instance in AWS.

For example:

OS: Ubuntu
Instance type: t2.micro / t3.micro

Keep your .pem key because you need it once to initially connect and enable password authentication.

In the EC2 Security Group, allow:

Type: SSH
Port: 22
Source: My IP

Note:-

for this particular lab, you should still create/select a key pair when creating the EC2 instance.

The reason is that a new Ubuntu EC2 instance normally does not have password SSH login enabled. You need the key once to get into the server and configure password authentication.

Step 2 — Connect to EC2 from WSL using the .pem key

From WSL:

chmod 400 mykey.pem

Then:

ssh -i mykey.pem ubuntu@YOUR_EC2_PUBLIC_IP

Example:

ssh -i mykey.pem ubuntu@13.233.100.50

You should now be inside the EC2 server.

Check:

whoami

Output:

ubuntu

Step 3 — Create a password for ubuntu user

Inside EC2:

sudo passwd ubuntu

It will ask:

New password:
Retype new password:

Set a password that you will remember for this lab.

For example, don't literally use this in a real environment:

Ubuntu@123

Step 4 — Enable SSH password authentication

Edit SSH configuration:

sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config

Find:

PasswordAuthentication no

Change it to:

PasswordAuthentication yes

Also check whether this exists:

KbdInteractiveAuthentication no

For this lab, that's okay.

Save:

Ctrl + O
Enter
Ctrl + X

Step 5 — Restart SSH

Run:

sudo systemctl restart ssh

Check:

sudo systemctl status ssh

You should see:

active (running)

Step 6 — Test password SSH from WSL

Exit EC2:

exit

Now you're back in WSL.

Test:

ssh ubuntu@YOUR_EC2_PUBLIC_IP

Example:

ssh ubuntu@13.233.100.50

This time you should get:

ubuntu@13.233.100.50's password:

Enter the password you created.

If you successfully enter the EC2 server, your password authentication is working.

Exit again:

exit
Don't use 0.0.0.0/0 for SSH unless you specifically need it for a temporary lab.
